            </div>
          </details>

          <!-- FAQ 2 -->
          <details open class="group bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-5 sm:p-6 open:border-teal-600 transition-colors shadow-sm">
            <summary class="flex items-center justify-between cursor-pointer list-none font-bold text-lg text-slate-900">
              <span>Which sleep position relieves lower back pain best?</span>
              <span class="ml-4 text-teal-700 font-mono text-xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="mt-4 text-slate-600 text-base leading-relaxed border-t border-slate-100 pt-4">
              Sleeping on your <strong class="text-slate-800">side with a firm pillow between your knees</strong> or sleeping flat on your <strong class="text-slate-800">back with a supportive pillow under your knees</strong> best relieves lower back pain. Both positions maintain the natural neutral curve of the lumbar spine and prevent overnight pelvic rotation. Avoid stomach sleeping, which hyperextends the lower back and strains cervical joints.
            </div>
          </details>

          <!-- FAQ 3 -->
          <details class="group bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-5 sm:p-6 open:border-teal-600 transition-colors shadow-sm">
            <summary class="flex items-center justify-between cursor-pointer list-none font-bold text-lg text-slate-900">
              <span>Can tight hamstrings cause lower back pain and poor posture?</span>
              <span class="ml-4 text-teal-700 font-mono text-xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="mt-4 text-slate-600 text-base leading-relaxed border-t border-slate-100 pt-4">
              Yes. Chronic <strong class="text-slate-800">hamstring tightness</strong> pulls downward on the ischial tuberosity of the pelvis, causing a posterior pelvic tilt that flattens the natural inward curve of the lumbar spine. This biomechanical shift increases compressive stress on lower lumbar discs and triggers compensatory upper-back slouching.
            </div>
          </details>

          <!-- FAQ 4 -->
          <details class="group bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-5 sm:p-6 open:border-teal-600 transition-colors shadow-sm">
            <summary class="flex items-center justify-between cursor-pointer list-none font-bold text-lg text-slate-900">
              <span>Are posture corrector braces safe to wear while working at a desk?</span>
              <span class="ml-4 text-teal-700 font-mono text-xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="mt-4 text-slate-600 text-base leading-relaxed border-t border-slate-100 pt-4">
              Passive posture corrector braces should only be worn for short <strong class="text-slate-800">20-to-30-minute intervals</strong> as a sensory reminder to avoid slumping. Prolonged daily reliance weakens deep postural stabilizers and thoracic extensors, worsening long-term back pain once the brace is removed.
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
